  <title>GIT commands</title>
  
<text xml:space="preserve"><note-content version="0.1"><underline>GIT commands</underline>

<underline>Command SQL analogy</underline>
git add      Insert rows into a staging table
git commit   Commit a transaction
git push     Replicate to a remote server
git gc       VACUUM FULL / database compaction
git reflog   Transaction log
git reflog   expire Delete old transaction log entries


<underline>#basic commands to upload changes.</underline>
git status
git log --oneline --graph
git add .
git status
git commit -m " "
git status

#see commit not yet uploaded
git log origin/main..HEAD --oneline

<underline># Check repository health</underline>
git status
git count-objects -vH

<underline># Safety net</underline>
git branch backup-before-clean

<underline># Go back to the last good commit while keeping your files</underline>
git reset --soft 618ac33

<underline># Unstage everything</underline>
git restore --staged .

<underline># After removing large files from history (not for routine use)</underline>
git reflog expire --expire=now --all
git gc --prune=now --aggressive

<underline>#If I accidentally commit a 3 GB ISO...</underline>
git reset ...
#or
git rebase -i
#then
git reflog expire --expire=now --all
git gc --prune=now --aggressive






Aha — now we have the exact picture. And I need to correct one thing from my previous message: the history is not clean yet.

This line proves it:

* dd15e52 (HEAD -&gt; main) Reorganize dotfiles
* 430a8f6 (backup-before-clean) kernel 7.1.8, waybar css minimal details
* 4778980 (origin/main) ...

And this is the smoking gun:

git log --all --oneline -- kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

dd15e52
40f52e0
430a8f6

The bad 151 MB file is still part of 430a8f6.

The fact that dd15e52 deletes it doesn't help. GitHub will still receive the blob from 430a8f6.

Also, your backup-before-clean branch is preserving the bad commit, which is fine — that's exactly what we want as a safety net.

And one very important thing

Your stash is empty:

git stash list

No output.

That's actually okay because your working tree is now clean. Your previously unstaged changes apparently ended up represented in dd15e52, so we don't have to worry about recovering them from the stash.

Let's do this properly now

We're going to edit 430a8f6 itself.

You currently have:

4778980  ← GitHub
    ↓
430a8f6  ← BAD: contains 151 MB kernel archive
    ↓
dd15e52  ← current commit, deletes it

We want:

4778980  ← GitHub
    ↓
NEW-HASH ← same as 430a8f6, but WITHOUT kernel archive
    ↓
NEW-HASH ← Reorganize dotfiles

Your backup-before-clean branch means we can always get back to the old state.

Step 1 — start the interactive rebase

Run:

git rebase -i origin/main

You should get two commits:

pick 430a8f6 kernel 7.1.8, waybar css minimal details
pick dd15e52 Reorganize dotfiles

Change only the first pick to edit:

edit 430a8f6 kernel 7.1.8, waybar css minimal details
pick dd15e52 Reorganize dotfiles

Save and exit.

Step 2 — Git should stop at 430a8f6

Now check:

git status

It should say something along the lines of:

You are currently editing a commit while rebasing...

And the kernel archive should now physically exist again, because Git has checked out the contents of 430a8f6.

Confirm:

ls -lh kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

You should see approximately:

151M kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz
Step 3 — remove it from this commit

Now this command should work:

git rm kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

Then:

git commit --amend --no-edit

You should see the commit recreated with the deletion.

Step 4 — continue the rebase
git rebase --continue

Git will replay your Reorganize dotfiles commit on top of the newly rewritten 430a8f6.

Then STOP

Don't push yet.

Run:

git log --oneline --graph --decorate -5

and:

git log --all --oneline -- kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

The second command should ideally show only the backup-before-clean commit:

430a8f6 backup-before-clean ...

and not your new main commits.

That's the critical verification.

Why this time will work

Earlier, we accidentally edited the new commit, which already contained the deletion:

dd15e52
delete mode ... linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

So there was nothing for git rm to remove.

This time we're explicitly stopping at:

430a8f6

where the 151 MB file actually exists.

Then we remove it from that historical snapshot.

After that, the later Reorganize dotfiles commit will still contain the deletion naturally.

Don't run git gc yet either. Once we verify the rewritten history, we'll make the old 151 MB object unreachable and then garbage-collect it.

b9205ab (HEAD -&gt; main) Reorganize dotfiles
ce45cb9 kernel 7.1.8, waybar css minimal details
4778980 (origin/main, origin/HEAD) MILK IN A WISKEY GLASS WITH A FLOATING PLUM theme complete, I think.
084ceef several modifications to theme, now more whitey-wlogout, fuzzel, snappy hypttasking, eye-candy. Reestructuration of style config. Divi$
ac7417b installed wl-clip-persist service, cliphist, compiled waybar (added cava support) and separated config into 2 jsonc files, one for eac$

40f52e0 (safety-before-rewrite) Reorganize dotfiles

430a8f6 (backup-before-clean) kernel 7.1.8, waybar css minimal details

safe to add commit and push?

Yes — now you're in the good state. 🎯

Your history is exactly what we wanted:

4778980  ← origin/main (GitHub)
   │
   ▼
ce45cb9  ← rewritten kernel commit
   │
   ▼
b9205ab  ← rewritten Reorganize dotfiles
   │
   ▼
HEAD

And importantly, your old commits are isolated on safety branches:

430a8f6  ← backup-before-clean
40f52e0  ← safety-before-rewrite

Those old commits are not part of main.

One final verification before pushing

Run:

git log --oneline --all -- kernel/7.1.8/linux-7.1.8.tar.xz

If you get:

430a8f6 (backup-before-clean) ...

and nothing involving b9205ab or ce45cb9, that's perfect.

Then:

git status

should say:

Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 2 commits.
nothing to commit, working tree clean
Then clean the old 151 MB object

Since main no longer references it, now is the right time:

git reflog expire --expire=now --all
git gc --prune=now --aggressive

Check:

git count-objects -vH

Then:

git push
</note-content></text>
